==German==

===Etymology 1===
From {{inh|de|gmh|hase|has(e)}}, from {{inh|de|goh|haso}}, from {{inh|de|gmw-pro|*hasō}}, from {{inh|de|gem-pro|*hasô}}, from an {{der|de|ine|-}} root originally meaning [[grey]].

====Pronunciation====
* {{IPA|de|/ˈhaːzə/}}
* {{IPA|de|/ˈhaːsɛ/}} {{q|Austria}}
* {{rhymes|de|aːzə|s=2}}
* {{audio|de|De-Hase.ogg|Audio}}

====Noun====
{{de-noun|m.weak|dim=^chen,^lein|f=^in[for the animal]}}

# [[hare]] {{gloss|animal of either sex}}
# {{lb|de|constellation}} the [[constellation]] [[Lepus]]

=====Usage notes=====
* While English speakers tend to mistakenly use the word “rabbit” for hares, the German tendency is the reverse: ''Hase'' is sometimes mistakenly used instead of {{m|de|Kaninchen}}, and it tends to be the preferred word whenever the distinction is irrelevant or impossible to tell (for example, a [[bunny girl]] is a {{m|de|Häschen}} in German and the [[Easter bunny]] is called {{m|de|Osterhase}}).

